\paragraph{Result}
The ensemble's own intervals are slightly too narrow (\RsevPar{} coverage at \(k=3\) for a nominal 0.90).
After calibration, coverage is \RsevCov{} \RsevCI{} and is on target at every \(k\), in every stratum and at every
level: at \(k=3\), {{ conformal_r7.json : results.alpha_0.2.k3.conformal.coverage | .3f }} for nominal 0.80 and
{{ conformal_r7.json : results.alpha_0.05.k3.conformal.coverage | .3f }} for nominal 0.95 (\cref{tab:r7,fig:calibration}).
Calibration has a price that is itself informative: intervals are wider for cytotoxic than for non-cytotoxic
chemicals ({{ conformal_r7.json : results.alpha_0.1.k3.by_regime.cytotoxic.width | .2f }} vs
{{ conformal_r7.json : results.alpha_0.1.k3.by_regime.non-cytotoxic.width | .2f }} vehicle SD at \(k=3\); indeterminate
chemicals are as wide), because network responses there collapse abruptly, and all intervals narrow as doses are added. The abstention rule flags
\RsevAbst\% of forecasts; their curve MAE is {{ conformal_r7.json : abstention.curve_mae_abstained | .3f }}, against
{{ conformal_r7.json : abstention.curve_mae_retained | .3f }} for retained forecasts, and the abstention rate falls from
{{ conformal_r7.json : abstention.by_k.1.rate | pct .1f }}\% at \(k=1\) to {{ conformal_r7.json : abstention.by_k.3.rate | pct .1f }}\% at \(k=3\).

\paragraph{Reading}
Coverage is a measured property inside the calibration domain (rat cortical NFA, chemical-level exchangeability),
not a guarantee that transfers to other species, cell sources or chips. Within that domain, a user can read a
twin interval at face value and can see when the twin declines to answer.

