is the most efficient gate: it stops
{{ r11_early_exit.json : methods.persistence.inactive_exit_rate | pct .0f }}\% of the inactive chemicals at
DIV\,7 while {{ r11_early_exit.json : methods.persistence.sensitivity | pct .0f }}\% of the active ones continue.
\NT{}, trained only with full-trajectory contexts, stops
{{ r11_early_exit.json : methods.twin.inactive_exit_rate | pct .0f }}\% (difference
\sgn{ {{ r11_early_exit.json : primary.diff_inactive_exit_rate | .2f }} }
\ci{ {{ r11_early_exit.json : primary.ci95.0 | .2f }} }{ {{ r11_early_exit.json : primary.ci95.1 | .2f }} }). By the
preregistered rule, persistence is the recommended throughput gate. An independent verifier recomputed every
score. The reference shares the DIV\,5/7 wells with every score, so R11b also evaluates every
method against a reference built from DIV\,9/12 only.
The external EPA DNT labels reverse the ranking where it matters most for safety: persistence would stop
{{ report_secondary.json : r11_epa_labels.positive.persistence_vs_twin_upper90.events_a }} of {{ report_secondary.json : r11_epa_labels.positive.persistence_vs_twin_upper90.n }} known neurotoxicants at DIV\,7, the twin's upper 90\% band
{{ report_secondary.json : r11_epa_labels.positive.persistence_vs_twin_upper90.events_b }} ({{ report_secondary.json : r11_epa_labels.positive.persistence_vs_twin_upper90.only_a }} against {{ report_secondary.json : r11_epa_labels.positive.persistence_vs_twin_upper90.only_b }} discordant chemicals,
exact McNemar \(p={{ report_secondary.json : r11_epa_labels.positive.persistence_vs_twin_upper90.exact_p | .3f }}\); a secondary, post-hoc test), while both stop a similar number of
the {{ report_secondary.json : r11_epa_labels.negative.persistence_vs_twin_upper90.n }} negatives ({{ report_secondary.json : r11_epa_labels.negative.persistence_vs_twin_upper90.events_a }} and {{ report_secondary.json : r11_epa_labels.negative.persistence_vs_twin_upper90.events_b }}). The system therefore
offers two gates: persistence for throughput, and the twin's upper band when a missed neurotoxicant is the
costlier error ({{ r11_early_exit.json : methods.twin_upper90.overall_exit_rate | pct .0f }}\% of chemicals stop).

\paragraph{Reading}
Most of the signal the complete assay will show is already present at DIV\,7, which makes an early-exit step
practical for a neural-OoC screening lab: with persistence,
{{ r11_early_exit.json : methods.persistence.overall_exit_rate | pct .0f }}\% of all chemicals stop after two of
the four recordings. The circularity control and the external labels below qualify the choice of gate. The
primary twin was never trained to extrapolate in time, which explains its lower efficiency.

\paragraph{R11b --- retraining the twin to extrapolate in time}
A second protocol, registered after R11 and before any training, changed one thing: during training, the
context wells lose DIV\,9/12 with probability one half, while the targets stay complete. Retraining raised the
twin's inactive exit rate to {{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.reference_active.methods.twin.inactive_exit_rate | pct .0f }}\%
(AUROC {{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.reference_active.methods.twin.auroc | .3f }}; paired gain over v1
\sgn{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.reference_active.twin_r11b_vs_twin_v1.diff_inactive_exit_rate | .2f }} }
\ci{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.reference_active.twin_r11b_vs_twin_v1.ci95.0 | .2f }} }{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.reference_active.twin_r11b_vs_twin_v1.ci95.1 | .2f }} }),
still below persistence
(\sgn{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : primary.diff_inactive_exit_rate | .2f }} }
\ci{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : primary.ci95.0 | .2f }} }{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : primary.ci95.1 | .2f }} }).
Against the circularity control, a reference built from DIV\,9/12 only
({{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.late_reference_active.n_inactive }} inactive chemicals), every early score
loses ground and persistence still leads: it stops
{{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.late_reference_active.methods.persistence.inactive_exit_rate | pct .0f }}\% of
the inactive chemicals against
{{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.late_reference_active.methods.twin.inactive_exit_rate | pct .0f }}\% for the
retrained twin, whose gain over v1 disappears there
(\sgn{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.late_reference_active.twin_r11b_vs_twin_v1.diff_inactive_exit_rate | .2f }} }
\ci{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.late_reference_active.twin_r11b_vs_twin_v1.ci95.0 | .2f }} }{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : references.late_reference_active.twin_r11b_vs_twin_v1.ci95.1 | .2f }} }).
Retraining did not harm dose interpolation (\(k=3\) curve MAE
\sgn{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : noninferiority_dose_interpolation.rel_change_pct | .1f }} }\%,
\ci{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : noninferiority_dose_interpolation.rel_change_ci95_pct.0 | .1f }} }{ {{ r11b_early_exit.json : noninferiority_dose_interpolation.rel_change_ci95_pct.1 | .1f }} }\%),
so the primary model remains the delivered forecaster. For a screening lab the recommendation is an auditable
gate at DIV\,7: persistence (the DIV\,7 network carried forward stays below the activity threshold) when throughput
matters most, or the twin's upper band when a missed neurotoxicant is the costlier error; retraining the twin for
the time dimension did not change that choice.

