\section{Cross-assay corroboration (X2, X3): details}\label{app:cross}

Both analyses are \emph{unpaired}: different plates, cultures, exposure windows and readouts. No parameter is
fitted on the external data.

\paragraph{X2: network formation versus neurite morphology (Harrill imaging)}
On the {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.n_overlap_primary }} chemicals matched by exact name or synonym, a chemical that
alters neurite or synapse morphology is almost always active in the NFA
(\(P={{ x2_harrill.json : headline.p_nfa_active_given_harrill_morph_active | .3f }}\)), whereas many NFA-active
chemicals leave morphology unchanged; activity agreement is {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.activity_agreement | .2f }}
\ci{ {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.activity_agreement_ci95.0 | .2f }} }{ {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.activity_agreement_ci95.1 | .2f }} }
(\(\kappa={{ x2_harrill.json : headline.kappa | .2f }}\)). Among the {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.n_both_active }}
chemicals active in both, potencies correlate (Spearman {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.spearman_potency_both_active | .2f }}
\ci{ {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.spearman_ci95.0 | .3f }} }{ {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.spearman_ci95.1 | .3f }} })
and the network BMC is lower by a median of {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.potency_offset_nfa_minus_morph_log10_median | abs .2f }}
log\(_{10}\) units \ci{ \sgn{ {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.potency_offset_ci95.0 | .2f }} } }{ \sgn{ {{ x2_harrill.json : headline.potency_offset_ci95.1 | .2f }} } }:
network function is altered at lower concentrations than morphology (\cref{fig:cross}a). The \NT{} forecast from
three concentrations preserves this ranking (Spearman {{ x2_harrill.json : twin_k3_vs_harrill_primary_overlap.potency_spearman_both_active.spearman | .2f }}
\ci{ {{ x2_harrill.json : twin_k3_vs_harrill_primary_overlap.potency_spearman_both_active.ci95.0 | .2f }} }{ {{ x2_harrill.json : twin_k3_vs_harrill_primary_overlap.potency_spearman_both_active.ci95.1 | .2f }} }),
although it calls more chemicals active than the morphology assay. The direction of change agrees no more often
than expected by chance ({{ x2_harrill.json : headline.direction_concordance_rate | .2f }} vs
{{ x2_harrill.json : headline.direction_expected_under_independence | .2f }}).

\paragraph{X3: developmental versus acute exposure (Kosnik)}
On {{ x3_kosnik.json : headline.n_overlap_measured }} overlapping chemicals, NFA developmental activity predicts
the published acute-MEA call above chance (balanced accuracy {{ x3_kosnik.json : measured_acute_call.nfa_reference.balanced_accuracy | .3f }}
\ci{ {{ x3_kosnik.json : measured_acute_call.nfa_reference.balanced_accuracy_ci95.0 | .3f }} }{ {{ x3_kosnik.json : measured_acute_call.nfa_reference.balanced_accuracy_ci95.1 | .3f }} };
the twin from three concentrations: {{ x3_kosnik.json : measured_acute_call.twin_k3.balanced_accuracy | .3f }}), with high
sensitivity but low specificity ({{ x3_kosnik.json : measured_acute_call.nfa_reference.specificity | .2f }}). Potency ranks
do not transfer (Spearman {{ x3_kosnik.json : measured_acute_call.potency_spearman_both_active.spearman | .2f }}
\ci{ \sgn{ {{ x3_kosnik.json : measured_acute_call.potency_spearman_both_active.ci95.0 | .3f }} } }{ {{ x3_kosnik.json : measured_acute_call.potency_spearman_both_active.ci95.1 | .3f }} },
one-sided \(p={{ x3_kosnik.json : measured_acute_call.potency_spearman_both_active.perm_p_one_sided | .3f }}\)). The
literature truth set overlaps the cohort in {{ x3_kosnik.json : truth_set.nfa_reference.n_positive }} neuroactive but
only {{ x3_kosnik.json : truth_set.nfa_reference.n_negative }} negative chemicals and is not interpreted. Acute
neuroactivity and developmental disruption are different questions; the agreement corroborates shared
neuroactive chemistry, not a DNT prediction.

